Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

Merchandise handles the common product data and behavior.
Book, EBook, and Magazine each handle their own type-specific details.
ShoppingBasket is responsible only for basket operations.
PaymentProcessor is responsible for payment/receipt logic.
InfoPrinter is responsible for display output.
This separation is a strong SRP pattern.
Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

You created small, focused interfaces instead of one large interface:
Displayable
Stockable
Payable
Each class implements only what it actually needs.
Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

Book, EBook, and Magazine all extend Merchandise and can be used anywhere a Merchandise is expected.
They all override getDisplayInfo() consistently, so polymorphism works as intended.
Open/Closed Principle (OCP)

Merchandise is abstract, and new product types can be added by extending it.
This means you can add more item types without rewriting the base logic.
However, InfoPrinter.printMerchandise() still uses instanceof, so this is only partially OCP-friendly.
Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

High-level logic depends on abstractions like Merchandise, Displayable, etc., rather than concrete classes.l
Example: basket and printer logic work with product abstractions, not hardcoded implementations.
